00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:08.560
And

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.740
number two is not something that I think most
of our viewers and listeners would expect

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:13.740
at this point.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:17.730
I'm going to praise Donald Trump because he
did, uh, did something that I agree with.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:21.570
This doesn't happen often, but when it does,
I tell you right away partially because it's

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:22.810
so notable.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:27.689
You know, those ads that you see on television
for medications, the do you feel tired in

00:00:27.689 --> 00:00:28.689
the morning?

00:00:28.689 --> 00:00:33.050
Well, if so you might be suffering from x
and y and here's a pill that might help you

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:38.660
with that and here's 30 side effects up to
and including death that uh, you might suffer

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.239
from if you decide to take this pill.

00:00:41.239 --> 00:00:45.949
Those are the ads that starting as soon as
this summer, big Pharma will be required to

00:00:45.949 --> 00:00:52.290
actually state the price of medications that
they are advertising in their commercials

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:54.239
for Donald Trump announced this week.

00:00:54.239 --> 00:00:57.570
This is going to apply specifically to any
drug.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:01.090
We're a monthly supply costs more than 35
bucks.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:07.479
We're all very used to hearing the side effect
list of side effects that the drug might cause.

00:01:07.479 --> 00:01:13.460
It's usually super sped up narration in the
last like five seconds of the ad.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:18.580
That's because of a government regulation
which said you must list the possible side

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:19.580
effects.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:20.650
Uh, in these commercials.

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:25.880
This is going to be a similar provision where
the price of the drug will have to be included

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:27.400
in those advertisements.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.750
This is a good thing.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:31.880
Good for Donald Trump for doing it.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:36.399
This is now like the second thing that Trump
has done that I can remember that I agree

00:01:36.399 --> 00:01:42.390
with the first being, uh, in his tax reform,
he reduced the amount of medical spending

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:48.040
at which point it becomes deductible, which
I think is a good thing here is good thing

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.290
number two, but there's actually a much bigger
issue here.

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:57.289
Big Pharma shouldn't even be allowed to market
drugs in this way directly to consumers and

00:01:57.289 --> 00:01:59.829
in a whole bunch of countries.

00:01:59.829 --> 00:02:04.130
It's not legal for big Pharma to market drugs
directly to consumers in this way.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:08.920
Direct marketing of drugs to patients is very
profitable for big Pharma.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:15.820
Um, it leads to patients actually going in
and asking for a particular drug of their

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:16.820
doctors.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:19.060
And a lot of countries don't allow this.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:24.720
And in fact I know New Zealand does or at
least did allow this type of direct to consumer

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.570
pharmaceutical marketing.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:31.340
But I had trouble finding other wealthy western
countries that allow these types of pharmaceutical

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:37.540
commercials on a math, you know, large reach,
corporate media, uh, marketing particular

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:39.540
drugs to individuals.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:43.930
And the pharmaceutical companies will always
say we do this because we want patients to

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:49.260
know things are out there and ultimately their
doctor's is going to be the one to decide

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:50.580
is it the right thing for them?

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:51.790
Is it not the right thing for them?

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:55.950
We're not actually pushing drugs to people,
but we know that that's not the case.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:03:01.440
We know that doctors sort of lean in the direction
of drugs that patients propose to them.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:07.760
We know that in the same way doctors are influenced
by the gifts and the being wined and dined

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.630
and shuttled around on different trips by
pharmaceutical companies.

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:14.870
And the proof is in the reality that the companies
keep doing it right?

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:18.330
If pharmaceutical companies realized, Oh,
you know what?

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:22.480
We're spending all this money on doctors wining
and dining and giving them gifts and trips

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:27.090
and dinner and they're not prescribing any
more of our medications, they would immediately

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:30.700
stop because they're for profit companies
and they're not going to do something that's

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:32.120
not good for their profits.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:39.520
In the same way, if pharmaceutical companies
believed or realized or noticed that when

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:45.760
they market drugs direct to consumers on television,
radio, wherever, that it has no effect on

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:49.440
the amount of prescriptions that are actually
filled.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.810
They would immediately stop doing it because
it would be a waste of money.

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:58.190
They know that it works, and once again, we
were talking about this yesterday.

00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:04.090
Lots of the ways that the US is unique aren't
good things.

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:10.440
We are third in the world for climate denial
behind Saudi Arabia and Indonesia among a

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.850
group of 25 countries that were surveyed.

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:15.400
It's not an achievement to be proud of.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:22.110
We are one of the few wealthy Western countries
that allows advertising for medications on

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:24.720
television directly to consumers.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:30.530
This is not a distinction that we should be
proud of and to be super thorough, there are

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:36.470
some studies which show that there is a net
benefit to society and to the economy for

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:38.700
advertising for antidepressants on television.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:40.750
Okay, so this is complex.

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:46.840
I'm against these types of ads as a general
rule, but I have read some studies which say

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:52.880
that when you have advertising for antidepressants
on television, on the one hand, usually the

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:58.790
ones being advertised are the ones that have
not gone generic and that are the most profitable

00:04:58.790 --> 00:04:59.790
ones.

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:04.010
So yes, you have this impact where people
see an ad for a particular antidepressant

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:06.370
and they go to their doctor and that's the
one that they asked for.

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:09.520
And we can argue that that's not necessarily
in the patient's best interest.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:15.660
It'd be better if the doctor without any influence
from the patient evaluated the available medications

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:17.440
and decided which one was best.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:24.420
But there are pure reviewed studies which
say that when you have a direct to consumer

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:31.370
television advertising for antidepressants,
it actually helps to get people who are depressed

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:36.580
on antidepressants who otherwise would not,
and that there's a measurable societal benefit

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:37.580
to that.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:40.020
There's a measurable economic benefit.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:42.550
It reduces absenteeism at work.

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:48.120
For example, depression is connected with
other medical conditions which can be expensive

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:49.120
to treat.

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:52.170
It reduces those by virtue of getting more
people on antidepressants.

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:57.520
So I think that if that's the case, if we
believe that those studies are accurate, you

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:04.430
can achieve a lot of that with more general
sort of nonprofit ads, which say, uh, if you

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:09.860
have these symptoms, there are medications,
talk to your doctor, your doctor can recommend

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:15.360
something that could be useful probably in
combination with psychotherapy, for example.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:20.330
So you could probably get a lot of the benefit,
the societal benefit, the general benefit

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:25.500
that there is for people who are suffering
from depression, seeking treatment for it

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:31.389
without allowing individual for-profit, big
pharmaceutical companies to market the most

00:06:31.389 --> 00:06:34.290
profitable pills that they have directly to
consumers.

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:36.430
But anyway, good for Trump, right?

00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:38.200
I mean it's a small victory.

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:42.860
